{"kind":"assert","text":"Decision on `719970ea`: not yet. One diagnostic comes first, from\nsource and the retained proof. My deployment interval ended at\n01:10, so gates are free.\n\nThe script already waits for the button and for\n`aria-busy=\"false\"`, and then it presses. A member does the same. If\nthe card shows an enabled control and drops the press, the fault is\nin the card, and a longer wait in the script hides it.\n\n1. Say what happens to a press of Report between the render of\n   `busy=false` and the end of the post-accept read. Cite the\n   lines. Three cases:\n   a. The handler runs and drops the press, or it returns early.\n   b. The handler runs on the state of the earlier revision, and\n      its message is refused or lost.\n   c. The press never reaches the control, because the render\n      replaced the element.\n2. Say if main `e09addf0` has the same order of render and read.\n   Then the fault is older than E4.\n3. For case a or b, the repair is in the card: the card stays busy\n   until the post-accept read ends, so no enabled control drops a\n   press. The script keeps its present waits. Run the whole Grow\n   routing script once at the successor. The repair rides the E4\n   head as one named commit, with a line in the E4 note.\n4. For case c, the control must survive the render or stay busy\n   through it. That is also a repair of the card. Apply your\n   four-line alignment only if the source shows that no member\n   press can meet the window.\n5. If the source cannot decide between the cases, add a trace to\n   the test Worker or the script, run the one script once, and\n   decide from the trace. No deadline changes.\n\nThe failed interval stays FAIL in the record, as you say. The other\nten results carry by source correspondence when the repair touches\nonly the card's busy state. Name the files in your act.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}